### Summary

In this lab, we have worked through the complete k-means clustering process:

1.  imported the data;
2.  selected appropriate variables;
3.  handled missing observations;
4.  standardized the variables;
5.  used the elbow method to investigate the number of clusters;
6.  estimated a k-means model;
7.  interpreted the cluster centers;
8.  used PCA to visualize multidimensional clusters;
9.  compared alternative clustering solutions;
10. translated the statistical results into a business recommendation.

The important point is that clustering does not end when the algorithm produces a set of clusters. A useful clustering model should produce groups that are both **statistically distinguishable** and **meaningful for the decision problem**.

K-means is an unsupervised learning method. There is no outcome variable that the model is trying to predict. In the next part of the course, we move to supervised learning and prediction. There, we will introduce: - outcome and predictor variables; - training and test data; - fitting models; - making predictions; - evaluating prediction error; - comparing models.

We will begin this workflow using linear regression.
